---
title: 'Fills & Borders'
---

This section describes how to change the fills and borders of chart elements.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Fills & Borders" name="fills-borders" type="generated" /%}

In the above example:

- A border is drawn around the series area with a corner radius and no padding.
- A fill and corner radius are applied to the chart title and subtitle, with a border applied to the title only.
- A border, fill and corner radius are applied to the [Legend](./legend), with padding between the border and items provided as a single number.
- A border, fill and corner radius are applied to the series labels and [axis labels](./axes-labels) with padding defined for each of the top, right, bottom and left.
- An [item styler](./stylers/#item-stylers) is used to override the styling of series labels for names starting with the letter 'D' and 'E'.

## Fills

```js format="snippet"
{
    label: {
        color: '#333', //font colour
        fill: '#badaff', // fill colour
        fillOpacity: 0.8,
    },
}
```

See [Series Fills](./fills) for details of the available fills types, including [patterns](./fills/#patterns) and [gradients](./fills/#gradients).

## Borders

```js format="snippet"
{
    border: {
        enabled: true,
        stroke: '#2c79d5',
        strokeWidth: 1,
    },
}
```

## Line Dash

The `lineDash` option sets the dash pattern of a stroke. It accepts either a named stock pattern or an array of numbers, and the names are accepted everywhere `lineDash` is.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Line Dash Patterns" name="line-dash-patterns" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'line',
            xKey: 'quarter',
            yKey: 'revenue',
            strokeWidth: 2,
            lineDash: 'long-dash-dot', // same as [14, 3, 2, 3]
        },
    ],
}
```

Each name is shorthand for a pattern of alternating dash and gap lengths, measured in multiples of the `strokeWidth`. A dot is always as long as the line is wide, so a name looks the same on a thin line as on a thick one.

| Name                    | Lengths                    | At `strokeWidth: 2`   |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------- | --------------------- |
| `'solid'`               | `[0]`                      | `[0]`                 |
| `'dot'`                 | `[1, 1.5]`                 | `[2, 3]`              |
| `'dash'`                | `[3, 2]`                   | `[6, 4]`              |
| `'dash-dot'`            | `[3, 1.5, 1, 1.5]`         | `[6, 3, 2, 3]`        |
| `'long-dash'`           | `[7, 2]`                   | `[14, 4]`             |
| `'long-dash-dot'`       | `[7, 1.5, 1, 1.5]`         | `[14, 3, 2, 3]`       |
| `'long-dash-dot-dot'`   | `[7, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2]`       | `[14, 4, 2, 4, 2, 4]` |
| `'dash-dot-dot'`        | `[3, 1.5, 1, 1.5, 1, 1.5]` | `[6, 3, 2, 3, 2, 3]`  |
| `'dash-long-dash'`      | `[3, 2, 7, 2]`             | `[6, 4, 14, 4]`       |
| `'long-dash-dash-dash'` | `[7, 1.5, 3, 1.5, 3, 1.5]` | `[14, 3, 6, 3, 6, 3]` |

A custom pattern is still given as an array of lengths in pixels, for example `lineDash: [8, 2, 2, 2]`, and is never scaled. The `lineDashOffset` is in pixels too.

Legend items and tooltip markers are drawn with the same dash as the series. The legend line is at most `2` pixels wide, so a named dash on a thicker series is drawn there at the series' dash lengths.

A name takes the `strokeWidth` set alongside it, so a [Styler](./stylers/) that returns both a `strokeWidth` and a `lineDash` name scales the name by that width. A highlight or selection style that changes only the `strokeWidth` keeps the dash lengths of the series, as it sets no `lineDash` of its own.

## Padding

Padding can be defined as a single number to be applied to all sides of the element, or as an object with values for `top`, `right`, `bottom` and `left`.

```js format="snippet"
{
    padding: 4, //padding of 4px on all sides
}
```

```js format="snippet"
{
    padding: {
        top: 4,
        right: 6,
        bottom: 2,
        left: 6,
    },
}
```

### Item Styler

Individual axis and series labels can be customised using a [Styler](./stylers/#item-stylers) callback.

```js format="snippet"
itemStyler: (params) => {
    if (params.datum.name.startsWith('D')) {
        return {
            /* ... */
            fill: {
                type: 'gradient',
                rotation: 90,
                colorStops: [{ color: '#c56600', stop: 0 }, { color: '#fb9323' }],
            },
        };
    }
}
```

